WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.610
We now have a dynamic
programming algorithm

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.260
that's going to be able to reconstruct
ancestral states in binary trees.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:14.250
But this algorithm assumes that
the structure of the tree was given.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:19.110
For example, here was an optimal solution
to the small parsimony problem for our

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.320
toy multiple alignment of four species,
if we're dealing with unrooted trees.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.419
But it assumes that chimp and
human are neighbors and that seal and

00:00:26.419 --> 00:00:27.500
whale are neighbors.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:32.840
It seems unlikely, but
there's nothing preventing chimp and

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.350
seal from being neighbors instead.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.968
We need to find the smallest parsimonious
score for this unrooted tree as well,

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:41.370
which is 11.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.770
We also need to find the smallest
parsimonious score for

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:48.190
the tree in which chimp and whale are
neighbors, where that score is also 11.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:52.633
Now I'll have you note that when applying
our algorithm for small parsimony,

00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:56.753
we're going to allow an internal edge
to have weight 0, as shown here.

00:00:56.753 --> 00:00:59.331
Because if we were to compress
this edge, then we would no

00:00:59.331 --> 00:01:00.630
longer have a binary tree.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.840
So this brings us to
the large parsimony problem

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.250
in which we must find an assignment
of ancestral states to internal nodes

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.630
over all possible unrooted
binary tree structures.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.500
As you might guess,
this problem is NP-Complete.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.950
So, let's design a greedy heuristic for
this problem.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.080
First, I want you to think about removing
an internal edge of an unrooted binary

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:30.880
tree, i.e.,

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.810
an edge that connects two internal nodes
along with the nodes that it connects.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.880
So for example,
if we delete the edge connecting a and

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.290
b here along with the nodes a and

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:45.520
b themselves, then we can see that
we have disconnected this unrooted,

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.640
binary tree into four, disjoint subtrees,
that I'll denote W, X, Y, and Z.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.720
There are three ways to then rearrange
these four subtrees around that internal

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:58.900
edge that connected a and b.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.380
So the operation in which we move
from one of these trees to the other

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.390
is called a "nearest neighbor interchange"
for this internal edge connecting a and b.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.990
To test your knowledge of this operation,

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:16.540
you might like to implement a program
that returns the two nearest neighbors of

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:20.680
a given unrooted binary tree with
respect to a given internal edge.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:27.259
This program will then be used for the
"nearest neighbor interchange heuristic" for

00:02:27.259 --> 00:02:28.753
the large parsimony problem,

00:02:28.753 --> 00:02:34.572
which begins with an arbitrary unrooted
binary tree, then performs all possible

00:02:34.572 --> 00:02:40.223
nearest neighbor interchanges over every
possible internal edge of the tree,

00:02:40.223 --> 00:02:45.310
then solving the small parsimony
problem for each such unrooted tree.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:51.290
Now, if we find a tree whose minimum
parsimony score improves over the current

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:57.190
tree that we have, then we set the current
tree equal to this tree, and we iterate.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:01.940
Otherwise, we can't improve on the current
tree via a nearest neighbor interchange,

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:03.590
and we simply return the current tree.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.200
Try implementing this greedy
heuristic yourself before we move on

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.750
to the final section of this chapter.

